 Node classification is a fundamental task in graph learning, where node attributes and relationships jointly support prediction. Graph neural networks (GNNs) combine these sources of information by aggregating representations from neighboring nodes \cite{ref1,ref2}. Their dependence on graph structure, however, creates practical difficulties at deployment. Predictions require access to the relevant neighborhoods, while retrieving and aggregating multi-hop neighbor information adds computational cost. In applications with missing relations, privacy restrictions, or low-latency requirements, this access may be unavailable or impractical. These constraints have motivated graph-free node classification, in which graph structure is used during training and the deployed classifier receives only node attributes. Graph-MLP \cite{ref3} and GLNN \cite{ref4} demonstrated this approach, and later work such as SimMLP \cite{ref41} and FF-G2M \cite{ref45} refined which structural knowledge is transferred to the MLP and how. These methods have been developed and evaluated with clean training labels.
 
-Noisy training labels pose an additional problem. Labels obtained through manual annotation, automated rules, or multiple sources may contain errors, and deep models can fit corrupted annotations \cite{ref5}. On graphs, message passing may propagate the influence of mislabeled nodes to their neighbors \cite{ref6}. Noise-robust graph methods use topology to augment supervision \cite{ref7}, to regulate training with noisy labels \cite{ref8}, or to select reliable samples \cite{ref9}. These methods require graph access at inference. For a graph-free classifier the problem is different: errors in the labels used to train the MLP, or its teacher, are carried into the deployed model, and transferring structural knowledge does not by itself correct them. This paper studies how the training graph can be used to repair noisy supervision for a classifier that uses only node attributes at inference.
-
-Our approach supplements each observed label with the labels of nearby training nodes. When most of these labels agree, pooling them reduces the influence of an isolated annotation error; neighbor-consistency methods for noisy labels outside the graph setting rest on the same principle \cite{ref10,ref11}. Two requirements follow. The groups must be formed without the observed labels, since otherwise annotation errors would shape both the groups and the supervision derived from them; a graph contrastive representation learned from node attributes and connectivity provides such a label-free basis \cite{ref12}. In addition, a node must not vote on its own target, since otherwise a corrupted label would reinforce itself.
-
-Granular ball computing describes a data set by a small number of hyperspherical regions, each summarized by a center and a radius, and generates them by recursive splitting until a quality criterion is met \cite{ref13,ref14}. Later work applied granular balls to sample selection under label noise \cite{ref15} and made their generation adaptive \cite{ref16}. Three properties of this representation match the requirements above. The balls form a disjoint cover, so all training nodes in a ball share one set of class counts, and the target of a node is obtained by removing its own vote from these counts. The number of balls is determined by the data under a prescribed budget, and each ball contains at least a minimum number of nodes, which prevents small regions in which a few corrupted labels would dominate the consensus \cite{ref15}. Existing generation methods split a ball when its label purity is too low \cite{ref13,ref15,ref16}; here the split is decided by a dispersion criterion that reads no labels, so the partition is fixed before any label is aggregated.
-
-We propose Granular Ball Local-Consensus Soft Supervision (GB-LCSS) for transductive node classification with noisy labels and graph-free inference. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1}, GB-LCSS first learns node representations through label-free graph contrastive learning and freezes them. It then recursively applies 2-means to the node representations in the frozen space, assigning each node to exactly one granular ball. For each training node, the labels of the other training nodes in its ball provide a local consensus, and a weight determined by the agreement among these labels blends the consensus with the node's observed label to form a soft target. These targets train an MLP that receives only node attributes at inference.
+Noisy training labels pose an additional problem. Labels obtained through manual annotation, automated rules, or multiple sources may contain errors, and deep models can fit corrupted annotations \cite{ref5}. On graphs, message passing may propagate the influence of mislabeled nodes to their neighbors \cite{ref6}. Noise-robust graph methods use topology to augment supervision \cite{ref7}, to regulate training with noisy labels \cite{ref8}, or to select reliable samples \cite{ref9}. These methods retain graph access at inference. Graph-free classifiers face a related challenge: errors in the labels used to train an MLP or its teacher can carry into the deployed model. We study how the training graph can improve noisy-label supervision for a classifier that uses only node attributes at inference.
+
+One way to address this challenge is to combine an observed label with evidence from nearby training nodes. When their labels provide consistent evidence for the underlying class, aggregating them can reduce the influence of isolated annotation errors. Neighborhood-based methods for noisy-label learning have exploited related forms of local consistency \cite{ref10,ref11}. For graph data, a graph contrastive representation learned from node attributes and connectivity without class labels provides a basis for identifying such local groups \cite{ref12}. The groups can then supply label evidence while their membership is determined independently of the observed labels.
+
+Granular ball computing provides a regional representation for this purpose. It groups samples into coarse-grained units summarized by their centers and radii \cite{ref13,ref14}. Subsequent studies have investigated granular ball sampling for noisy or imbalanced classification \cite{ref15} and refined adaptive ball generation \cite{ref16}. Depending on the task, ball construction can be guided by label purity or geometric criteria. Geometry-based construction is suitable here because it allows local groups to be formed before the training labels are aggregated. In our construction, each node belongs to exactly one group, and the regional class statistics can be reused across its members' targets. A dispersion criterion adapts the number of groups within a fixed budget, with a minimum size required for each accepted child group.
+
+Building on this idea, we propose Granular Ball Local-Consensus Soft Supervision (GB-LCSS) for transductive node classification with noisy labels and graph-free inference. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1}, GB-LCSS first learns node representations through label-free graph contrastive learning and freezes them. It then recursively applies 2-means to the node representations in the frozen space, assigning each node to exactly one granular ball. For each training node, the labels of the other training nodes in its ball provide a local consensus, and a weight determined by the agreement among these labels blends the consensus with the node's observed label to form a soft target. These targets train an MLP that receives only node attributes at inference.
 
