 The main contributions are summarized as follows:
 
 \begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
-\item We study noisy-label node classification under graph-free inference, a setting in which graph structure is available during training but the deployed classifier receives only node attributes. Existing graph-free methods have been evaluated with clean training labels, and existing noise-robust graph methods keep the graph in the prediction path.
-\item We propose GB-LCSS, which constructs training targets in two decoupled steps. A frozen, label-free graph contrastive representation is partitioned into disjoint granular balls by a dispersion criterion that reads no labels, and each training node's target blends its observed label with the consensus of the other training nodes in its ball, weighted by the concentration of that consensus. We bound the probability that the local vote favors a wrong class under independent symmetric label noise and derive an exact decomposition of the squared error of the soft target.
-\item We show on five datasets and five noise settings that GB-LCSS attains the highest or tied-highest mean accuracy in 24 of 25 settings among seven noisy-label baselines with the same MLP, that its targets have lower Brier scores than the observed labels in every setting, that the gain vanishes under random grouping and under generic label smoothing, and that the same targets train a graph teacher whose distilled student improves on four GNN-to-MLP distillation routes.
+\item We introduce GB-LCSS for noisy-label node classification with graph-free inference. It organizes nodes into granular balls in a frozen, label-free graph representation, separating regional grouping from noisy-label aggregation.
+\item We construct node-specific soft targets by combining each observed label with the consensus of other training nodes in its ball. Under independent symmetric label noise, we analyze how the number of votes and the underlying class distribution affect the reliability of local voting. We also derive an exact decomposition of the soft target's squared error.
+\item We evaluate GB-LCSS on five datasets and five noise settings against noisy-label and graph-assisted baselines. Controlled comparisons examine grouping, representation learning, target construction, and parameter sensitivity, while a graph-teacher extension evaluates the same targets for distillation.
 \end{enumerate}
 
 The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. \hyperref[sec:2]{Section~\ref*{sec:2}} reviews related work, and \hyperref[sec:3]{Section~\ref*{sec:3}} presents GB-LCSS, analyzes local-consensus supervision, and introduces the graph-teacher extension. \hyperref[sec:4]{Section~\ref*{sec:4}} describes the experimental setup, comparative results, ablations, extensions, and discussion. \hyperref[sec:5]{Section~\ref*{sec:5}} concludes the paper.
