 \subsection{Discussion}\label{sec:4.5}
 
-The experiments locate the source of the gain. Randomizing the ball membership removes it entirely, and generic smoothing recovers none of it (Table~\ref{tab:4}), so the gain comes from the labels of the specific training nodes that share a ball. Two design decisions make this evidence usable under label noise. The balls are formed from a label-free representation, so corrupted annotations cannot shape them, and each node is excluded from the vote that adjusts its own target, so a corrupted label cannot reinforce itself. Proposition~\ref{prop:local_vote} bounds the probability that the vote favors a wrong class under independent symmetric noise and a positive clean-class margin, and Appendix~I.2 shows that the reduction in target error on corrupted labels exceeds the increase on correct labels in every run, by a factor of at least 6.7 in the five-seed means. The Brier-score analysis (Fig.~\ref{fig:4}) confirms that the targets themselves, not only the trained classifier, move toward the clean labels in all 25 settings.
-
-The targets change only in balls whose training nodes disagree. With clean training labels, disagreement arises only in balls that contain several classes, and accuracy then differs from MLP+CE by at most 2.00 points in either direction (Appendix~D); the method is intended for training sets in which label errors are expected. Its margin over the strongest noisy-label baseline varies with the dataset (Appendix~G), whereas its margin over random grouping is present on every dataset evaluated, which is the comparison that isolates the contribution of local membership.
-
-Within graph-free node classification, GB-LCSS acts on the training targets, Graph-MLP and GSDN act on the representation through the training loss, and GNN-to-MLP distillation acts through a teacher's predictions. These mechanisms can be combined. The teacher extension replaces the observed labels with GB-LCSS targets and improves the distilled student in nine of ten settings while leaving the deployed MLP unchanged, and the same targets can be used with a Graph-MLP objective.
-
-Granular balls organize the consensus into shared regions. Each node belongs to exactly one ball, the class counts of a ball are reused for every target constructed within it, the number of balls is determined by the data under the prescribed budget, and every ball contains at least \(n_{\min}\) nodes. The sensitivity analysis (Section~\ref{sec:4.3.4}) shows that the only settings with large accuracy losses are those in which the partition collapses to a single ball or the coverage of consensus supervision drops substantially, both of which are visible from the partition before any label is read. kNN and k-means partitions of the same representation give accuracies within one point on four of the five datasets (Table~\ref{tab:5}), which shows that the consensus rule, rather than the specific partitioning procedure, carries the gain; the granular ball partition is retained because it provides these structural guarantees at a lower storage cost than per-node neighborhoods on the larger graphs (Fig.~\ref{fig:5}).
-
-The main experiments use synthetic label noise on fixed transductive graphs with clean validation labels for model selection; Appendix~C shows that corrupting the validation labels leaves the method ranking essentially unchanged on three datasets. Real annotation errors and inductive prediction for nodes unseen at training time are natural next settings for local-consensus supervision.
+GB-LCSS improves supervision by combining each observed label with evidence from related training nodes. The label-free representation determines which nodes contribute to this evidence, and excluding the current node's vote keeps its annotation out of its own consensus estimate. When the other labels provide consistent evidence for the underlying class, their consensus can offset an isolated error. The advantage over random size-matched grouping and generic label smoothing supports the importance of choosing these contributing nodes (Table~\ref{tab:4}). The target-quality analysis adds direct evidence: the soft targets have lower mean Brier scores in all 25 settings, and the error reduction on corrupted labels exceeds the error introduced on correct labels in the decomposition reported in Appendix~I.2.
+
+The usefulness of local evidence depends on both its class composition and the number of available votes. Proposition~\ref{prop:local_vote} relates these quantities to voting reliability under independent symmetric noise. Agreement alone is insufficient when the local labels favor an incorrect class, since the consensus can then move the target away from the true class. The representation and parameter studies show lower accuracy when local groups fail to form or voting support becomes sparse. With clean training labels, the refinement improves one dataset and lowers accuracy on four, with all changes within 2.00 percentage points (Appendix~D). On the additional noisy-label datasets, it exceeds random grouping throughout but the strongest baseline on three of six datasets (Appendix~G). These findings place the benefit in the quality of local label evidence, which varies across datasets and supervision conditions.
+
+GB-LCSS, Graph-MLP, and GLNN share an attribute-only deployment interface but use the training graph differently. Graph-MLP incorporates a neighborhood-contrastive objective, and GLNN transfers predictions from a GNN teacher. GB-LCSS uses a label-free graph representation to identify related training nodes, then combines their observed labels into soft supervision. The comparison in Table~\ref{tab:3} places it alongside these alternative training strategies. The teacher extension demonstrates one way to combine target refinement with distillation: replacing the observed labels with GB-LCSS targets for the same GCN teacher improves the student's mean accuracy in nine of ten settings. The contribution therefore concerns supervision for graph-free prediction, with benefits for both direct MLP training and graph-to-MLP transfer.
+
+Granular balls provide a practical organization for this supervision. Each node is assigned to exactly one group, and the regional statistics are shared across its training-node targets. Geometric splitting determines the number of groups within a fixed budget and enforces a minimum child-group size. Alternative grouping rules also support local-consensus targets: their mean accuracies are within one percentage point on four datasets, while kNN performs better on CoraFull (Table~\ref{tab:5}). Granular ball grouping requires less stored structure than exact kNN on the three larger graphs and has the shortest construction time on three datasets (Fig.~\ref{fig:5}). Its value is the combination of competitive accuracy, reusable regional statistics, and controlled grouping cost.
 
 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:5}
 
-This paper addressed node classification with noisy training labels when the deployed classifier receives only node attributes. GB-LCSS uses the training graph for a single purpose, to repair the supervision. A label-free graph contrastive representation is frozen and partitioned into disjoint granular balls by a dispersion criterion that reads no labels, and each training node's target blends its observed label with the consensus of the other training nodes in its ball, weighted by how concentrated that consensus is. The classifier trained on these targets is an ordinary MLP.
-
-The experiments support three statements. First, the soft targets are closer to the clean labels than the observed labels in every one of the 25 dataset--noise settings, and the MLP trained on them attains the highest or tied-highest mean accuracy in 24 of 25 settings among seven noisy-label baselines with the same architecture, without knowledge of the noise rate. Second, the gain comes from which nodes share a ball: randomizing the membership while preserving the ball sizes removes the gain in all 25 settings, and replacing the consensus with uniform or class-prior smoothing recovers none of it. Third, the mechanism is not tied to one partitioning procedure; kNN and k-means partitions of the same representation give accuracies within one point of the granular ball partition on four of five datasets, and the granular ball partition is retained because it provides shared class counts, a data-determined number of regions under a fixed budget, and a minimum region size at a lower storage cost than per-node neighborhoods. The same targets also train a graph teacher whose distilled student exceeds four GNN-to-MLP distillation routes in eight of ten settings.
-
-Future work will extend the target construction to inductive prediction for nodes unseen at training time, to model selection with noisy validation labels, and to consensus weights that account for the number of votes as well as their agreement.
+We presented GB-LCSS for learning from noisy training labels when the deployed classifier receives only node attributes. The method organizes nodes into granular balls in a frozen, label-free graph representation and combines each observed label with the consensus of other training nodes in its ball. The resulting soft targets use the training graph to improve supervision while preserving graph-free inference. Our analysis relates local-vote reliability to the available votes and their clean-class margin under symmetric noise, and decomposes the squared error of the soft target.
+
+Across five datasets and five noise settings, GB-LCSS achieves or ties for the highest five-seed mean accuracy in 24 of 25 settings among seven noisy-label baselines using the same deployed MLP architecture. Its soft targets have lower mean Brier scores than the observed one-hot labels in all 25 settings. Controlled comparisons support representation-based regional supervision, with granular balls providing a compact way to share label statistics. The graph-teacher extension further shows that the same targets can improve the supervision transferred to an MLP through distillation.
+
+The present evaluation focuses on synthetic label noise in a fixed-graph transductive setting, with clean validation labels used for model selection. Future work will examine whether local consensus remains effective with real annotation errors, develop model-selection procedures for settings with limited reliable validation labels, and extend region construction to newly arriving nodes.
 
